	<key>readme</key>
	<string>Run an ad-hoc shell command from Alfred without opening a Terminal window.

Keywords: `run &lt;cmd&gt;` (or `&gt; &lt;cmd&gt;`)

Commands run via a headless `zsh -i -l`, so `[[ -o interactive ]] || return` guards in your .zshrc still fire and `ZDOTDIR` is respected. Init-time stderr is silenced (to hide the "can't change option: zle" noise that tty-less `-i` triggers from many setups); the command's own stderr is still captured via a separate fd. Aliases, functions, and PATH from your dotfiles are all available. Working directory is $HOME, so relative paths like `Desktop/foo.txt` work as expected.

After running:
- stdout is copied to the clipboard (if non-empty)
- a macOS notification shows the exit status and a truncated preview

Notes:
- If the `&gt;` keyword collides with Alfred's built-in "Terminal command" feature, disable it in Alfred Preferences → Features → Terminal/Shell.
- Alfred.app may need Full Disk Access or other TCC permissions if your commands touch protected paths.
- GUI-launched shells don't inherit tmux/terminal session env. Stuff set only in `.zshenv` on the currently focused terminal is fine (.zshenv always loads), but tmux-specific vars won't be present.</string>
